kz-relations 1
rules relations rules revision 4 (2026-10-30)
mode profile-free
  extract.builtin_profile = phi 1; profile "kz-static-builtin-minimal"; (profile hash in the header)
  extract.profile = kz-static-builtin-minimal / 
  extract.p0_table = P0 table revision 3 (2026-10-30), 72 entries
  extract.p0_table_scope = families of this revision: thread creation, mutex and spin lock, semaphore, condition variable, sleep, termination of the calling thread or process, thread identity, thread-specific data, cleanup handlers, dynamic loading (open and symbol lookup), data functions; with the ISO C11 <threads.h> members of those families and the glibc 2.39 *_clock* members.  Of a family the members that take, release, wait, signal, sleep, end, name a thread, bind or create a key, push or pop a handler, load or look up, or store memory are entries; a family is not listed whole where this list names members left out.  Not in this revision: read-write locks (pthread_rwlock_*; their shared acquisitions are not read by the lock-set analysis, so the family is left out whole and its acquisitions are no lock operations), barriers (pthread_barrier_*), one-time initialization (pthread_once, call_once), thread cancellation of another thread (pthread_cancel and the cancellation state functions), signals (kill, raise, pthread_kill, sigwait), dlclose, dlerror and dladdr, functions a compiler or a fortify wrapper calls without a call in the source (__stack_chk_fail, __chk_fail and the *_chk functions); thread join and detach (pthread_join, pthread_tryjoin_np, pthread_timedjoin_np, pthread_detach, thrd_join, thrd_detach); POSIX timers (timer_create, timer_settime, timer_gettime, timer_getoverrun, timer_delete; a timer with SIGEV_THREAD notification starts its function on a new thread, which is no actor here); I/O multiplexing (select, pselect, poll, ppoll); the initialization, destruction, attribute and query members of the mutex, spin lock, semaphore and condition variable families and of thread creation (pthread_mutex_init, pthread_mutex_destroy, pthread_mutexattr_*, pthread_spin_init, pthread_spin_destroy, sem_init, sem_destroy, sem_open, sem_close, sem_unlink, sem_getvalue, pthread_cond_init, pthread_cond_destroy, pthread_condattr_*, mtx_init, mtx_destroy, cnd_init, cnd_destroy, pthread_attr_*); scheduling (sched_*, pthread_setschedparam, pthread_getschedparam, pthread_setschedprio); data functions not in this revision (stpcpy, stpncpy, strlcpy and strlcat (glibc 2.38 and later), mempcpy, memccpy, explicit_bzero, and the wide-character members); formatted input, which stores through its arguments (sscanf, vsscanf, fscanf, vfscanf, scanf, vscanf); handlers registered for process exit (atexit, at_quick_exit, on_exit)
  extract.derivation_rules = wrapper derivation rules F7/F8 revision 1 (2026-10-30)
  extract.targets_rule = R-T1,R-T2 (target rules revision 1): the targets of the second pass, chosen from the first pass's ARGFLOW (R-T1) and READS with RETURNS (R-T2), shared bases only (a global object, a global array element, the pointee of a global pointer or of a pointer field reached from a global)
p0-compare P0 comparison table revision 2 (2026-10-30): memcmp (byte-string comparison; POSIX.1-2017 / glibc 2.39; source unverified) strcmp (byte-string comparison; POSIX.1-2017 / glibc 2.39; source unverified) strcoll (byte-string comparison; POSIX.1-2017 / glibc 2.39; source unverified) strncmp (byte-string comparison; POSIX.1-2017 / glibc 2.39; source unverified) strcasecmp (byte-string comparison; POSIX.1-2017 / glibc 2.39; source unverified) strncasecmp (byte-string comparison; POSIX.1-2017 / glibc 2.39; source unverified) strcoll_l (byte-string comparison; POSIX.1-2017 / glibc 2.39; source unverified) strcasecmp_l (byte-string comparison; POSIX.1-2017 / glibc 2.39; source unverified) strncasecmp_l (byte-string comparison; POSIX.1-2017 / glibc 2.39; source unverified) bcmp (byte-string comparison; glibc 2.39; not in POSIX.1-2017 (removed in Issue 7); source unverified) strverscmp (byte-string comparison; glibc 2.39 (GNU); not in POSIX.1-2017; source unverified) pthread_equal (thread-id comparison; POSIX.1-2017 / glibc 2.39; source unverified) thrd_equal (thread-id comparison; ISO C11 <threads.h> / glibc 2.39; source unverified)
p0-compare scope: families listed whole: byte-string comparison (<string.h>, <strings.h>, with the locale _l members, bcmp and strverscmp), thread-id comparison (pthread_equal, thrd_equal).  Not in this revision: wide-character comparison (wcscmp, wcsncmp, wcscoll, wcscasecmp, wcsncasecmp, wmemcmp and their _l members) and __memcmpeq (called by a compiler without a call in the source)
